{"success": true, "name": "continual-arc-solving", "file": "references/demo-delta-discipline.md", "content": "# Demo-delta discipline

In a motif/recoloring ARC task, reason from exact input-output changes before inferring the rule. Record changed coordinates and old/new colors, along with unchanged components. Test candidate operations against every changed and unchanged cell; a visual resemblance between an embedded shape and another cluster is not enough evidence for stamping, copying, or recoloring.

After an incorrect submission, treat the feedback as rejection of the candidate rule. Avoid a minor variation of the same unsupported hypothesis. Request another demo when the remaining examples could distinguish plausible rules, and budget demos against the cost of another wrong submission. The final task attempt here was still unresolved, so this note captures only the validated procedural lesson, not a guessed transformation.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-hermesagentlearner-1792484802/attempts/57865763327545bc884e27f058eb3817/system/state/hermes/skills/reasoning/continual-arc-solving/references/demo-delta-discipline.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "continual-arc-solving", "file": "references/compact-output-interpretation.md", "content": "# Interpreting compact ARC outputs

When demonstrations map a larger grid to a small output, treat the output as a structured encoding rather than an arbitrary answer shape. For every demo, establish:

- what input region(s) correspond to output rows and columns;
- whether non-background markers are copied, recolored, aggregated, or filtered;
- how empty/background output cells are determined;
- whether partitions, separator lines, or repeated tiles define independent source regions.

Record the exact source-to-output mapping and check every output cell against multiple pairs. Similar output dimensions alone do not establish the encoding. Before using the rule on a test, derive the output cell-by-cell and verify dimensions. If plausible mappings disagree, use the next demo to distinguish them; do not submit a guessed uniform pattern merely to conserve attempts.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-hermesagentlearner-1792484802/attempts/57865763327545bc884e27f058eb3817/system/state/hermes/skills/reasoning/continual-arc-solving/references/compact-output-interpretation.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "continual-arc-solving", "file": "references/recurrent-variant-demo-discipline.md", "content": "# Recurrent task IDs and demo discipline

A task ID recurring in a lifelong ARC stream does not guarantee that the operation is unchanged; treat prior-instance rules as hypotheses, not answers. Recompute each current demo's exact input/output delta independently, including unchanged cells, before transferring a remembered rule to the test.

When demos are underdetermined, maintain concrete competing hypotheses and request examples that distinguish them. Do not spend a submission on a visual analogy or remembered rule unsupported by this instance. After a demo, incorporate its actual deltas before choosing another action. Preserve the exact action-only response contract throughout.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-hermesagentlearner-1792484802/attempts/57865763327545bc884e27f058eb3817/system/state/hermes/skills/reasoning/continual-arc-solving/references/recurrent-variant-demo-discipline.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "continual-arc-solving", "file": "references/recurrent-instance-overgeneralization.md", "content": "# Recurrent instances: avoid transferring unverified rules

Task IDs may recur while each instance supplies a fresh test grid and demonstrations. Treat prior-session/task-ID notes as candidate hypotheses only. Re-derive the motif and operation from current-instance input/output deltas before applying any remembered rule.

